                "word": "fétuque",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "f4191c89-a0da-4b72-b25c-5f85873f8594",
                        "entry_id": "ca077330-33c7-32a6-b89e-550e1e200738",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "fétuques"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "2b30acb2-b4fa-3e77-bf6c-acb62732986a",
                        "entry_id": "ca077330-33c7-32a6-b89e-550e1e200738",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Genre de plantes (Festuca) qui appartient à la famille des Poacées (anciennement graminées), comprenant de 300 à 400 espèces de vivaces rhizomateuses, à feuillage caduc ou persistant. Le genre est largement répandu dans toutes les zones tempérées et pousse dans les prés et les bois."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "313d74e4-66dc-4b83-a2fa-e7da747c5fbd",
                                "sense_id": "2b30acb2-b4fa-3e77-bf6c-acb62732986a",
                                "text": "Il confère à la fétuque une toxicité très forte pour certaines chenilles, marquée pour le bétail bovin et ovin qui en consomme beaucoup."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ddedcc53-8ff1-4032-b59f-9f44dcfcda34",
                                "sense_id": "2b30acb2-b4fa-3e77-bf6c-acb62732986a",
                                "text": "Le vent dispersait déjà les cendres de son feu. Les touffes de fétuque étaient aplaties là où il avait dormi. — (Daniel Nayeri, Duel à Toy Farm, dans Brigade des crimes imaginaires et autres histoires fantastiques et déglinguées, traduit de l’anglais par Valérie Le Plouhinec, Hélium, 2008, page 123)"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "11242303-7f19-44fa-8edd-120280f18634",
                        "source_entry_id": "ca077330-33c7-32a6-b89e-550e1e200738",
                        "source_sense_id": "2b30acb2-b4fa-3e77-bf6c-acb62732986a",
                        "source_sense_description": "festuca",
                        "target_lang_word": "fescue",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "fescue",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c70ba52d-3e6a-37e4-a588-8c04fd3d6a0c",
                                        "entry_id": "b20a904a-8614-3764-bc9c-c6409a7ed0f1",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A straw, wire, stick, etc., used chiefly to point out letters to children when learning to read."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b1e4c184-c4d4-46f5-b905-eb06b5aa6215",
                                                "sense_id": "c70ba52d-3e6a-37e4-a588-8c04fd3d6a0c",
                                                "text": "to come under the fescue of an imprimatur"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "70bc2d28-1651-447f-b9b4-5a6c201e7410",
                                                "sense_id": "c70ba52d-3e6a-37e4-a588-8c04fd3d6a0c",
                                                "text": "‘Now then,’ Mason rapping upon the Table’s Edge with a sinister-looking Fescue of Ebony, whose List of Uses simple Indication does not quite exhaust, whilst the Girls squirm pleasingly"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "28f90da8-f537-34d1-8054-027f880d2621",
                                        "entry_id": "b20a904a-8614-3764-bc9c-c6409a7ed0f1",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A hardy grass commonly used to border golf fairways in temperate climates. Any member of the genus Festuca."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "fc3ae62d-2abe-38dc-8697-76a32204004d",
                                        "entry_id": "b20a904a-8614-3764-bc9c-c6409a7ed0f1",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An instrument for playing on the harp; a plectrum."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d70a60d6-d96e-435f-bd5a-2b5fad7dd93a",
                                                "sense_id": "fc3ae62d-2abe-38dc-8697-76a32204004d",
                                                "text": "with thy golden fescue play'dst upon\nThy hollow harp"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "25e83c23-9709-3b8e-96df-4912f6014727",
                                        "entry_id": "b20a904a-8614-3764-bc9c-c6409a7ed0f1",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The style of a sundial."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "fescue",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3729694b-5f4e-3636-866b-eed895158e2a",
                                        "entry_id": "69876061-b47f-345e-8f5b-d43e6f73a653",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To use a fescue, or teach with a fescue."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7cb0c0fd-5e7c-4385-8a6b-20991f98488c",
                                                "sense_id": "3729694b-5f4e-3636-866b-eed895158e2a",
                                                "text": "A minister that cannot be trusted to pray in his own words without being chewed to, and fescued to a formal injunction of his rote lesson, should as little be trusted to preach, besides the vain babble of praying over the same things immediately again ; for there is a large difference in the repetition of some pathetical ejaculation raised out of the sudden earnestness and vigour of the inflamed soul, (such as was that of Christ in the garden,) from the continual rehearsal of our daily orisons;"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
